+# Review: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockShop.server.luau`
+
+Findings are ordered by impact. 1 to 3 can be exploited or lose player data,
+4 to 6 are bugs a normal player will hit, and the rest are maintainability and
+style. Line numbers refer to the file as it is now.
+
+The project's other files use `local` bindings and `@game/...` string
+requires, so the suggestions below keep those conventions.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Client arguments are trusted without checks (exploitable)
+
+**Where:** `buyRemote` handler (lines 84-103), `placeRemote` handler (105-125),
+`removeRemote` handler (127-140).
+
+**What's wrong:** The annotations `bt: string`, `amt: number`, `pos: Vector3`
+and `part: Instance` describe what an honest client sends. An exploiter can
+send anything, and the type checker can't see that.
+
+- **NaN gives free coins and unlimited blocks.** `amt = 0/0` passes
+  `amt < 1 or amt > MAX_PURCHASE` because every comparison with NaN is false.
+  Then `price` is NaN, `coins < price` is false, and `setCoins` writes NaN
+  (or the DataStore call errors). If the write goes through, `playerStock[bt]`
+  becomes NaN. On line 107 `(NaN) < 1` is false, so every later placement is
+  allowed, and `playerStock[bt] -= 1` leaves it at NaN. The result is
+  unlimited blocks of that type.
+- **Fractional amounts.** `amt = 1.5` passes and buys 1.5 blocks at a
+  fractional price.
+- **Unknown block type.** `PRICES[bt]` is nil for an unknown `bt`, so
+  line 89 throws. That's harmless but noisy. `placeRemote` never checks `bt`
+  either and relies only on the stock lookup.
+- **Bad positions.** A non-Vector3 `pos` makes `PlacementRules.snap` throw.
+  A Vector3 with NaN components gets through the height and reach checks in
+  `PlacementRules.check`, because those comparisons are false too, and may
+  place a block at a NaN position.
+- **Bad instances.** A non-Instance `part` throws on `part.Parent`.
+
+**Do instead:** Type every argument after `player` as `unknown` and narrow it
+before using it. `BlockTypes.isBlockType` already exists for the block type.
+Shape the number checks so they reject NaN:
+
+```lua
+buyRemote.OnServerEvent:Connect(function(player: Player, blockType: unknown, amount: unknown)
+	if not BlockTypes.isBlockType(blockType) then
+		return
+	end
+	if
+		typeof(amount) ~= "number"
+		or amount ~= math.floor(amount) -- rejects NaN and fractions
+		or amount < 1
+		or amount > MAX_PURCHASE
+	then
+		return
+	end
+	-- blockType :: BlockTypes.BlockType is now safe
+end)
+```
+
+For `pos`, check `typeof(pos) == "Vector3"` and that each component equals
+itself (`pos.X == pos.X` and so on) before snapping. For `part`, check
+`typeof(part) == "Instance"` and `part:IsA("BasePart")`.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Coins round-trip to the DataStore on every purchase, with no pcall (double-spend, throttling, lost purchases)
+
+**Where:** `getCoins` / `setCoins` (lines 42-48), called from the buy handler
+(lines 90-94).
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- **Race / double-spend.** `GetAsync` yields. Two `BuyBlocks` events fired
+  together both read the same balance (say 100), both pass the check, and both
+  write `100 - price`. The player pays once and gets stock twice. Any number of
+  parallel requests multiplies it.
+- **Request budget.** Each purchase costs one read and one write. A client
+  spamming the remote uses up the server's DataStore budget, and that throttles
+  saves for every player in the server.
+- **No error handling.** DataStore calls throw on outages and throttling.
+  If `SetAsync` throws after `GetAsync` succeeded, the handler dies halfway.
+  Nothing retries or tells the player.
+- **Naming.** Both functions yield but aren't marked `Async`, so their call
+  sites don't show the yield.
+
+**Do instead:** Load the balance once when the player joins (inside `pcall`,
+with retries) into an in-memory table. Have the buy handler read and write
+that table synchronously, with no yield between the check and the deduction,
+so the race goes away. Save on `PlayerRemoving`, on a periodic autosave, and in
+`game:BindToClose`, using `UpdateAsync` inside `pcall`. Name the functions
+`loadCoinsAsync` / `saveCoinsAsync`. If coins will be used by other systems
+later (rewards, other shops), move this into a dedicated data module rather
+than keeping it inside the shop.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Removing an orphaned block credits the remover's stock (free-block farm, currently hidden by bug 4)
+
+**Where:** line 139, together with the rule on lines 132-136.
+
+**What's wrong:** When a block's owner has left, anyone may remove it, which
+is the intended behavior. But line 139 then adds the block to the *remover's*
+stock. A player with an alt account can buy blocks on the alt, place them,
+leave, and collect them on the main account for free. Any player can also
+harvest every block an absent player built. Today bug 4 stops the "owner left"
+path from ever running, so fixing bug 4 alone would open this exploit.
+
+Removal also has no reach check. Placement requires the block to be within
+`PlacementRules.MaxReach`, but a player can delete their own blocks from
+anywhere on the map.
+
+**Do instead:** Credit stock only when the remover is the owner. Clearing an
+orphaned block just destroys it, or refunds the owner if stock ever becomes
+persistent. Add the same reach check that placement uses, measured from the
+remover's `HumanoidRootPart`.
+
+---
+
+## 4. `tostring(getOwnerName(part))` throws when the owner has left
+
+**Where:** `getOwnerName` (lines 50-57) and its call on line 133.
+
+**What's wrong:** When no player matches, `getOwnerName` falls off the end and
+returns *no values* (not `nil`). `tostring()` called with zero arguments throws
+"missing argument #1 to 'tostring'", so the "anyone can clear away a block
+whose owner has left" branch can't run. Even if it could, comparing names
+through the `"nil"` string is fragile.
+
+**Do instead:** Compare user IDs directly and drop the helper:
+
+```lua
+local ownerId = part:GetAttribute("OwnerId")
+local isOwner = ownerId == player.UserId
+local isOrphaned = typeof(ownerId) ~= "number" or Players:GetPlayerByUserId(ownerId) == nil
+if not isOwner and not isOrphaned then
+	return
+end
+```
+
+If a helper stays, give every path the same number of return values: end it
+with `return nil`.
+
+---
+
+## 5. `stock[plr]` can be nil, and the handlers crash when it is
+
+**Where:** `stock` declaration (line 40), uses on lines 96-97, 106-107 and
+139, and `PlayerAdded` (lines 142-144).
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- `PlayerAdded` is only connected at the bottom of the script. Any player who
+  joined before the script ran never gets an entry, and that happens regularly
+  in Studio Play Solo. Their first buy throws on `playerStock[bt]`.
+- A remote event queued just before `PlayerRemoving` runs after
+  `stock[plr] = nil` and throws.
+- The type `{ [Player]: { [string]: number } }` tells the checker every lookup
+  succeeds, so it can't flag either case.
+
+**Do instead:** Type it as `{ [Player]: { [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number }? }`
+so the checker forces a nil check. Guard each handler with
+`local playerStock = stock[player]; if not playerStock then return end`.
+After connecting `PlayerAdded`, loop over `Players:GetPlayers()` to set up
+anyone already in the server.
+
+Separately, stock isn't saved anywhere. Blocks a player bought but didn't
+place disappear when they leave, even though the coins were deducted and
+saved. Decide whether that's intended. If it isn't, save stock alongside coins
+(see finding 2).
+
+---
+
+## 6. Players can't turn receipts off
+
+**Where:** line 99.
+
+**What's wrong:** `plr:GetAttribute("ShowReceipts") or DEFAULT_SHOW_RECEIPTS`
+turns `false` into `true`, because `or` replaces `false` as well as `nil`.
+A player who sets `ShowReceipts = false` still gets receipts. The `AutoAnchor`
+code on lines 118-121 handles this correctly with `== nil`.
+
+**Do instead:** Use the same `== nil` pattern as `AutoAnchor`, or pull both
+into one small helper:
+
+```lua
+local function getBooleanSetting(player: Player, name: string, default: boolean): boolean
+	local value = player:GetAttribute(name)
+	if typeof(value) == "boolean" then
+		return value
+	end
+	return default
+end
+```
+
+This also guards against an attribute holding a non-boolean value.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Clients can flood the server output
+
+**Where:** `print` on line 79 (runs on every placement) and `warn` on line 114
+(runs on every rejected placement).
+
+**What's wrong:** Both lines run on every request with client-controlled
+input, so one client can spam the output. Every successful placement also
+prints by default, which buries real warnings during play.
+
+**Do instead:** Drop the `print`, or put it behind a debug flag. Make the
+rejected-placement case silent, or send the reason back to that player only
+so the client can show it.
+
+---
+
+## 8. `placeBlock` takes five positional arguments, two of them booleans
+
+**Where:** lines 59-82, called on line 124 as
+`placeBlock(plr, center, bt, autoAnchor, true)`.
+
+**What's wrong:** The trailing `true` doesn't say what it means at the call
+site, and `log` is a boolean that switches behavior. `bt :: BlockTypes.BlockType`
+on line 66 is a cast, so an invalid type gives `style = nil` and errors on
+line 72 instead of being rejected up front.
+
+**Do instead:** Once finding 7 removes the log, take the validated type and
+an options table:
+
+```lua
+local function placeBlock(
+	player: Player,
+	position: Vector3,
+	blockType: BlockTypes.BlockType,
+	options: { Anchored: boolean? }?
+): Part
+```
+
+---
+
+## 9. Prices live in the server script and are keyed by plain strings
+
+**Where:** `PRICES` (lines 14-20).
+
+**What's wrong:** A shop UI on the client will need the same prices, and
+keeping a second copy will drift. The `{ [string]: number }` type also lets a
+typo such as `Bricks = 5` through, and nothing flags a missing block type
+when one is added to `BlockTypes`.
+
+**Do instead:** Move prices into shared code, either as a `Price` field on
+each `BlockStyle` in `BlockTypes` or as a `BlockPrices` module beside it,
+typed `{ [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number }`. The annotation can then go,
+because inference covers the rest.
+
+---
+
+## 10. Style and typing
+
+These have lower impact but are worth doing while the file is open:
+
+- **No `--!strict`** on line 1. The shared modules have it, and several of the
+  bugs above (nil `stock` entries, missing return values) are the kind strict
+  mode helps catch.
+- **Abbreviated names:** `plr`, `amt`, `bt`, `p`, `pos`. Use `player`,
+  `amount`, `blockType`, `position`.
+- **Anonymous handlers:** name them after when they run (`onBuyBlocks`,
+  `onPlaceBlock`, `onRemoveBlock`, `onPlayerAdded`, `onPlayerRemoving`) and
+  connect them by name. The handlers get longer once validation is added, and
+  names make the bottom of the file easier to read.
+- **`getOwnerName`** has no return type (moot if finding 4 removes it).
+- **Remotes created at runtime** (lines 24-34): this works, but any client
+  script must `WaitForChild` for each one. Defining them in the Rojo project
+  (`default.project.json` or `.model.json` files) makes them exist before any
+  script runs and lets client code reference them statically. Optional.
+
+---
+
+## Suggested order of work
+
+1. Validate client input (1) and fix the `stock` nil handling (5). Both are
+   small and contained.
+2. Rework coin storage into a load-once, save-on-leave cache (2). This is the
+   largest change and the one with the most data-loss risk, so it's worth a
+   spec under `Tests/Specs` that covers the double-spend case.
+3. Fix removal ownership and refunds together (3 and 4), so the exploit in 3
+   never ships.
+4. Fix the small bugs (6, 7), then refactor (8-10).
